00:20:30.079 --> 00:20:35.040
definitely differentiates us, and I would, you know, kind of pitch ourselves to say,

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:40.800
please stop by at JSM to see this in person. We, along the way, we built our own learning

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:44.560
management system, which is not something that everyone that's doing content creation is doing,

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:49.920
and for those that don't know what that means, if you are currently in academia, you might be

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:54.719
using something like Blackboard or Canvas, and if you don't, and if you're not in academia,

00:20:54.719 --> 00:20:59.359
you might see things online where companies let you upload your own content, and there's nothing

00:20:59.359 --> 00:21:03.920
wrong with those companies. They're extremely efficient. They let you post a video and then

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:09.599
type HTML or just have widget-based things to go into it. We built our own, and the reason we

00:21:09.599 --> 00:21:14.959
built our own is because we wanted to be able to streamline true data science environments to

00:21:14.959 --> 00:21:21.199
learners. We are a data science-centric learning management system, and so we are organizing all

00:21:21.199 --> 00:21:29.439
of our content directly through that LMS. We are definitely leveraging a lot of AI for our notes

00:21:29.439 --> 00:21:35.760
at this point, but the way that our actual instruction goes, instructors are encouraged to

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:41.760
take their own teaching styles, create their content. We do have some standard operating

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:47.760
procedures for how they should submit things like code to us, but in general, those instructors

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:55.760
within their own disciplines are encouraged to organize their slides, their code, record their

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:59.680
videos, and then they send them to our team, and then we eventually put them onto our site, and

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:05.040
that's more for the courses. As far as how do we actually

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:11.119
outline a syllabus or a curriculum or something like that, we're working directly with the

00:22:11.119 --> 00:22:17.599
instructor. We are researching literally any university or public offering on the e-learning

00:22:17.599 --> 00:22:21.520
side that you can think of and making sure that we're meeting the standard of what students are

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:25.680
learning in college. We try to be extremely academic on our site while still being an e-learning

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:30.719
site, and so we are researching pretty much everything that exists at the time that the course

00:22:30.719 --> 00:22:36.719
is created. This kind of brings me to my next point of, you know, okay, as you create a course on

00:22:37.359 --> 00:22:44.239
say July 30th, what happens a month, a year from now? Do these courses go stale, and how do you

00:22:44.239 --> 00:22:49.839
manage that on the site? We just recently launched what we call Athletics Masterclasses or

00:22:49.839 --> 00:22:54.959
Athletics Live, and this is kind of what led into the partnership with Posit, was we

00:22:54.959 --> 00:23:01.119
needed to be able to spin up virtual environments within like five seconds. It takes

00:23:01.119 --> 00:23:08.160
a long time to do what we were doing for courses, and so we now have the ability to

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:15.520
take a zip file from an instructor where they are either teaching in R or Python. We drop it

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:22.319
onto our content management system, and then a virtual environment will deploy, and then it lands

00:23:22.319 --> 00:23:27.599
directly on the page where the instructor is teaching, and so it lets us deploy these really

00:23:27.599 --> 00:23:34.959
quickly. As far as how we track things like student progress on there, we have a bunch of

00:23:34.959 --> 00:23:41.280
different analytics tools on the site to track completion. If there's quizzes, student progress,

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:44.640
and things like that, but I think really the most important thing that we're doing

00:23:45.359 --> 00:23:53.199
for organizing all the content is we are making sure that the code runs before the session,

00:23:53.199 --> 00:23:59.520
and that's getting a little outside this question, but having taught both to students while

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:05.040
TAing or doing my own stuff on the consulting side, one thing that always comes up is

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:08.880
you might get a contact from a student, hey, my environment doesn't work, hey, I'm

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:14.719
